WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.950
So, five decisions, each with its owner.

2
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:07.720
The authorizing official determines
the boundary before workloads arrive,

3
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.925
and signs every exception
with an end or review date.

4
00:00:10.925 --> 00:00:15.446
The system owner keeps the boundary
record, gates every new subscription,

5
00:00:15.446 --> 00:00:19.225
and checks every feature dependency
before a design is built.

6
00:00:19.225 --> 00:00:24.613
The CISO names the reviewer of provider
change notices and ongoing certification

7
00:00:24.613 --> 00:00:29.463
reports, and decides what counts as
evidence, with the ISSO doing the reading.

8
00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:34.449
The sponsor and CIO fund the measurement
and the compensations the register calls

9
00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:39.075
for, and plan against the class you actually
have, not the one you're waiting for.

10
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:43.024
And the contracting officer buys
the SKU the boundary assumes,

11
00:00:43.024 --> 00:00:46.062
and specifies capabilities,
not program badges.

12
00:00:46.062 --> 00:00:47.975
The next step needs no award.

13
00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:50.242
Before the next architecture review,

14
00:00:50.242 --> 00:00:54.997
list every cloud feature your designs
depend on, with its Marketplace package,

15
00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:58.800
its availability in your environment
and the page that says so.

16
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.670
Then put Appendix A's
requests to your provider,

17
00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.025
and record the answers in your package.

18
00:01:04.025 --> 00:01:08.579
The rules become mandatory
on January 1, 2027,

19
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:12.763
and new Rev5 requests
stop on June 11, 2027.

20
00:01:12.763 --> 00:01:15.225
Neither date moves your own boundary.

21
00:01:15.225 --> 00:01:18.812
It's still yours to draw,
to evidence and to sign.

22
00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:22.812
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

23
00:01:22.812 --> 00:01:26.861
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 10 has the detail,

24
00:01:26.861 --> 00:01:28.724
with every claim footnoted.

25
00:01:28.724 --> 00:01:29.540
Thank you.
